      "message": "[Gerrit AI Review] ❌ **Changes Requested** — Please encapsulate the `USE_LEGACY_GPIO_API` guard inside `warn_gpio()` and add unit test coverage in `util/flash_fp_mcu_test.sh`. [[trajectory](https://goto.corp.google.com/traj/a44da871-b2dc-4da4-9de8-82a48e1be1b1)]\n\n### Review Summary\n* **Functionality:** ✅ Restricting the pre-flash `warn_gpio` check to `USE_LEGACY_GPIO_API\u003d1` prevents `gpioget` from reconfiguring `BOOT0` and `NRST` as inputs when using the character-device GPIO API without background hold processes (`GPIOSET_SUPPORTS_HOLD\u003d0`).\n* **Architecture \u0026 Component Fit:** ❌ Other GPIO helpers (`gpio()` and `gpio_chip_exists()`) encapsulate the `USE_LEGACY_GPIO_API` check inside the helper layer, and `_gpio_new()` treats legacy-only operations (`export`, `unexport`, `in`, `out`) as no-ops so `flash_fp_mcu_board()` can call GPIO primitives unconditionally. Moving the `USE_LEGACY_GPIO_API` check into `warn_gpio()` keeps this abstraction consistent and makes `warn_gpio()` directly unit-testable.\n* **Code Simplicity:** ✅ The condition itself is minimal and clear.\n* **Idiomaticity \u0026 Readability:** ✅ Matches existing Bash conventions in `util/flash_fp_mcu`.\n* **Security:** ✅ No security concerns.\n* **Testing:** ❌ Although `TEST\u003d` cites `flash_fp_mcu_test.sh`, no unit test was added to `util/flash_fp_mcu_test.sh` to verify that `warn_gpio` skips `gpio get` when `USE_LEGACY_GPIO_API\u003d0` and checks the pin state when `USE_LEGACY_GPIO_API\u003d1`. See the inline comment for a ready-to-use test snippet.\n* **CL Size \u0026 Scope:** ✅ `Size: XS (+4, -3)`. Single, focused logical change.\n* **Commit Message:** ✅ Clear, concise subject and body explaining why `warn_gpio` must be restricted to the legacy sysfs GPIO API.",
